WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.990
Layered stromatolites preserve the constructions of microbial communities.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:09.130
The source describes examples around three point five billion years old

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:12.130
and compares them with modern Australian stromatolites.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:17.230
Photosynthetic cyanobacteria helped transform the environment by supplying oxygen.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:22.710
Modern analogues clarify the structure without making today’s atmosphere identical to

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:23.470
the ancient one.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:28.410
Around the source’s Cambrian boundary, diverse animals with hard coverings became

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:30.290
widespread over millions of years.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:35.690
Trilobites, brachiopods and unusually preserved soft-bodied animals document this change.

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:40.250
The Cambrian explosion is biological diversification on a geological timescale.

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:42.930
It does not mean life first appeared then or that the

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:43.910
event was an eruption.

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:49.750
Early Paleozoic seas contained corals, crinoids, mollusks and trilobites.

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:54.610
Later, fishes became diverse while plants and insects occupied land.

00:00:54.890 --> 00:01:00.070
The artist’s source reconstructions represent interpretations of fossil evidence, rather than

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:02.150
photographs of a vanished ecosystem.

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:06.490
Compare the organisms and their environments instead of treating one scene

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:07.530
as the whole era.

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:13.850
Amphibians and early reptiles diversified during later Paleozoic time alongside land

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:15.870
plants, insects and marine life.

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:19.890
The era ended with the Great Dying, a major extinction affecting

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:24.650
oceans and land. The course describes environmental stresses and several proposed

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:30.110
causes; no single illustrative animation establishes which causal combination produced the

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:30.450
extinction.

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:37.830
The section discusses Siberian flood-basalt eruptions, a conjectural impact, changes associated

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.930
with Pangaea, and ocean overturn.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:45.070
Eruption ages overlap the extinction, while the proposed impact connection is

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:49.390
explicitly uncertain. Continental assembly altered seas and climate;

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:54.470
oxygen-poor water reaching shallow environments supplies another proposed mechanism.

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:57.450
Preserve each hypothesis and its evidence limits.
